	Parameter passing methods:
		Call by value:
			When you dont have to modify actual parameters, we use pass by value
			We use this when we want a function to process a value and return the result, without actually modifying the actual parameters.
		Call by address:
			In pass by address, the formal parameter should be of the type pointers.
			And the parameters passed to the function from main fucntion should of the type address.
			It uses pointers
			One function cannot access the variables of other functions, but it can do so using pointers
		Call by reference:
			Need to pass references of actual parameters to the external function. eg[a->&x,b->&y]
			When reference parameters are manipulated, the actual parameters are also modified
			reference is nothing but a nickname for the variable it is referencing
			
	Array as parameter:
		Array is passed as an address, they cannot be passed by value.
		The other parameter passed to the function represents the size of the array, which is passed by value.
		In this case, modifying the values in call function will modify the values in main function parameter, as it is called by address.
		Code:
			// void fun(int B[], int n) //method 1
			void fun(int *B, int n)     //method 2, 
			/*
			instead of using array like B[], 
			we can use *B. As this *B can point 
			to an integer as well as to an array.
			So using B[] also means a pointer to 
			an array
			*/
			{
				int i;
				for(int i=n-1; i>=0; i--)
				{
					printf("%d ", B[i]);
				}
			}
			
			int main()
			{
				int A[] = {2,4,6,8,10};
				fun(A,5); // here address of A is passed to the above function fun
				
				return 0;
			}
			
