	Structure as a parameter:
		Passing struct as a call by value:
			When we pass struct object as a parameter to some other function, a separate object is created by that function. It does not use the pre-existing object from main function. So only the formal parameters change, not the actual parameters.
		Passing struct as a call by reference:
			Compared to the above explaiantion, in this case separate object is not created, and the actual parameters are modified.
		Passing struct as a call by address:
			when we want a function to change the actual parameters, we use call by address or call by reference.
		Code:
			struct Rectangle
			{
				int length;
				int breadth;
			};
			
			int area_value(struct Rectangle r1)   //call by value, does not change the actual parameters
			{
				return r1.length*r1.breadth;
			}
			
			int area_reference(struct Rectangle &r1)  //call by address, changes the actual parameters
			{
				return r1.length*r1.breadth;
			}
			
			void change_length(struct Rectangle *r1, int l)  //call by address, changes the actual parameters
			{
				r1->length=l;                   //We use pointer to a structure, as change_length has *r1 passed from address in main function
				
			}
			
			int main()
			{
				struct Rectangle r = {10,5};
				
				change_length(&r, 20);    //passing struct as an address
				
				printf("area_value is %d \n", area_value(r));
				printf("area_reference is %d \n", area_reference(r));
				//printf("area_address is %d \n", area_address(r));
			
				return 0;
			}
		
