	Template Classes:
		code:
			
			template<class T>		//template starts here and ends at the next curly bracket
			class Arithametic
			{
				private:
					int a;
					int b;
				public:
					Arithametic(int a, int b);
					int add();
					int sub();
			}
			
			template<class T>  //as we are using class name as a scope resolution and passing it to template, we need to pass the template name through the class name as a part of scope resolution
			Arithametic<T>::Arithametic(T a,T b)
			{
				this->a=a;	//this is a pointer to the current object
				this->b=b;
			}
			
			template<class T>
			int Arithametic<T>::add()
			{
				T c;
				c = a+b;
				return a+b;
			}
			
			template<class T>
			int Arithametic<T>::sub()
			{
				T c;
				c=a-b;
				return a-b;
			}
			
			int main()
			{
				Arithametic<int> ar(10,5);  //here once we have declared the Arithametic type as int, it replaces all the places where we have typed "T"
				cout<<ar.add();
				
				Arithametic<float> ar1(1.5,1.2);
				cout<<ar1.add(); 
			}
					
	Linked_lists:
		Arrays have difficulty in updating, adding or deleting items in the list, the use of linked lists comes in place.
		Linked lists are inferior to array, as they are bad at searching, while in array we just need to find the address of the starting element.
		
		Singly linked lists:
			Lets consider each memory space where data is stored as a "NODE".
			So in arrays, each NODE has two components, one is the node data and 2nd is the node index. In arrays, all the nodes are in a contiguos manner.
			However, in linked lists, each node has a data and address of the next node. In this way, the nodes in a linked lists need not be in a contiguous manner.
			So whenever we need to update, add, delete any data in a linked list, we need not re-arrange all the data, we just need to update the address stored in the subsequent nodes.
			While in array, we need to move all the subsequent nodes while doing the same.
		
